# Suppressions for reachable memory that OpenSSL's own internal caches hold
# (RSA blinding state, BN_MONT_CTX Montgomery contexts, encoder and provider
# tables, and similar lazily-computed values that RSA key generation and
# signing fill in). Here it's the stream_tls test group of tests_parser that
# reaches them: it drives a real TLS handshake off a certificate that
# ctls_ctx_cert_add() generates in process, so the key generation happens
# inside libcrypto and not in a separate openssl(1) process.
#
# OpenSSL allocates these blocks through its own CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc
# wrappers, and libcrypto's internal static and thread-local state keeps
# them alive for the lifetime of the process. Only an explicit
# OPENSSL_cleanup() call releases them, and we don't make that call (calling
# it unconditionally at process exit isn't safe in a library meant to be
# embedded in a larger application, which may have other OpenSSL handles in
# use). So they aren't leaks; it's the same category that ld_so_dlopen.supp
# documents for glibc's own NSS module loading.
#
# The rules match the ORIGIN LIBRARY of the allocation and not a wrapper
# function name. Whether CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc/CRYPTO_strdup/
# CRYPTO_strndup/BN_MONT_CTX_new/OPENSSL_sk_deep_copy (and others) show up
# as their own frame between malloc/realloc and the rest of the stack, or
# are inlined away entirely, depends on how that particular libcrypto.so.3
# was built, which we don't control. What is always there is a caller of
# malloc/realloc that lives in libcrypto.so.3, and that's exactly what these
# rules require. A block that libssl asks for is covered by the same rules,
# because libssl allocates through CRYPTO_malloc, which lives in libcrypto.
#
# That frame is pinned to position one, right above the allocator, on
# purpose. A rule that only requires a libcrypto frame SOMEWHERE in the
# stack also matches a block that OUR code allocates while it runs
# underneath libcrypto, and ctls.c does that every time OpenSSL calls its
# SNI or ALPN callback. Such a block is ours to account for, so these rules
# leave it reported.
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_malloc_from_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_realloc_from_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:realloc
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
